This paper provides a methodological reanalysis of a public dataset (Hyder et al., 2025) investigating the bidirectional modulation of seizure onset via dorsal striatum optogenetics in a pilocarpine-induced status epilepticus (SE) model. Rather than claiming novel biological findings, the author demonstrates the utility of applying a time-to-event survival framework to seizure latency data, contrasting this with the original study's use of rank-based testing. ... Label-Permutation Test: A 2,000-shuffle permutation test was performed to establish an empirical null distribution for the log-rank statistic, providing a robust check on the significance of the findings given the sample size (n=8−11n=8-11n=8−11 per group).  ... - **ChR2 Activation:** Delays SE onset relative to GFP (median 692s vs. 466s; $$HR\approx 0.338$$
). The result showed moderate fragility in the LOO analysis, with 2 of 20 iterations failing to reach $$p<0.05$$
.
**ArchT Inhibition:** Accelerates SE onset relative to GFP (median 640s vs. 880.5s; $$HR\approx 9.18$$
). This comparison was highly robust across all sensitivity checks.

The author concludes that while survival analysis is not strictly necessary for samples lacking censored data, it serves as a superior, reusable, and interpretable template for secondary reanalyses. By providing effect sizes ($HR$) and documented sensitivity (LOO results), this methodological vignette highlights the importance of rigorous validation in small-sample neuroscientific datasets.
